(2747649, 379843, 31307, '<td valign="top">I tried to do something like this but found that my string burned too fast for the shot to happen.  What kind of string did you use, out of curiosity?  Congrats on the 33rd!</td>', 'I tried to do something like this but found that my string burned too fast for the shot to happen.  What kind of string did you use, out of curiosity?  Congrats on the 33rd!', '2006-08-22 00:58:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(2747760, 380021, 36988, '<td valign="top">So as expected, this image was hit fairly hard. I knew going in that it would be a stretch for some but it is an image I am happy about entering and in the end that''s what counts. Sure high placements are nice but...<br/>I spent quite a while getting my daughter to throw her hair back in a variety of speeds and ways. Took quite a few shots and many of them were really good. I chose this one because of the flamelike area at the top. It was a little soft from the motion but I think it really enhanced the impression of fire. <br/>Editing was simple.<br/>A little sat adjustment and some dodge and burn to enhance the lights and darks in the hair.<br/>Neat image helped with the smooth look.<br/>The cameo effect was achieved by using a faster than normal shutter speed with my studio flash. After trying out a few different settings, I discovered that the faster shutter speed did a nice job of isolating the face and hair. Lesson learned... don''t be afraid to break the rules and experiment.<br/>Thanks for all the great comments. They were greatly appreciated... :)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-08-22 00:55:13.</i></td>', 'So as expected, this image was hit fairly hard. I knew going in that it would be a stretch for some but it is an image I am happy about entering and in the end that''s what counts. Sure high placements are nice but...I spent quite a while getting my daughter to throw her hair back in a variety of speeds and ways. Took quite a few shots and many of them were really good. I chose this one because of the flamelike area at the top. It was a little soft from the motion but I think it really enhanced the impression of fire. Editing was simple.A little sat adjustment and some dodge and burn to enhance the lights and darks in the hair.Neat image helped with the smooth look.The cameo effect was achieved by using a faster than normal shutter speed with my studio flash. After trying out a few different settings, I discovered that the faster shutter speed did a nice job of isolating the face and hair. Lesson learned... don''t be afraid to break the rules and experiment.Thanks for all the great comments. They were greatly appreciated... :)', '2006-08-22 01:52:39.000000', '2006-08-22 01:55:13.000000', 0),
